(load "stdlib.writ")

; The questions about two-phase commit, kept beside the model and asked of every
; variant of it — which is the point of a claims file being a separate document.

; ── the safety property the protocol exists to provide ────────────────────────
; ATOMICITY: the parties finish together or not at all. Never one committed
; while another aborted. `never` is a census over every reachable situation, so
; a holding `never` is a proof and not a search that came up empty.
(property atomic "no participant commits while another aborts"
  (never (and (some (p participant) (is p.state committed))
              (some (q participant) (is q.state aborted)))))

; The protocol is worth running: committing is actually reachable, and a holding
; `possible` prints the route that gets there.
(property can-commit "the parties can all commit"
  (possible (all (p participant) (is p.state committed))))

; ── the liveness property it does NOT provide ─────────────────────────────────
; `live` asks whether deciding is STILL REACHABLE from every reachable
; situation. Where the coordinator can crash it is not, and the counterexample
; is two moves long: one participant prepares, the coordinator dies.
(property can-decide "from anywhere, every participant can still decide"
  (live (all (p participant)
    (or (is p.state committed) (is p.state aborted)))))

; `inevitable` asks the stronger thing, and the one "does this terminate"
; actually means: is there any run at all that never decides? A run avoids the
; goal by stopping short of it or by going round for ever, and both are
; failures. This is the property that separates the plain protocol from the
; retransmitting one, where `can-decide` HOLDS and this one does not.
(property must-decide "no run avoids deciding"
  (inevitable (all (p participant)
    (or (is p.state committed) (is p.state aborted)))))

; ── the same question, of the runs that actually happen ───────────────────────
; A run in which a participant is handed the decision and declines to apply it,
; again and again for ever, is not a run anyone would call a counterexample.
; `(fair …)` says so. The assumption is attached to THIS property rather than
; written into the model, so the unconditional question above survives beside
; it — and every verdict prints what it assumed.
(property must-decide-if-applied
  "…and no run avoids it, if a participant told the decision eventually applies it"
  (inevitable
    (all (p participant) (or (is p.state committed) (is p.state aborted)))
    (fair p1-commit p1-abort p2-commit p2-abort)))
